<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bon</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Bön</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bon is a Tibetan religious tradition that traces its teachings to the founder <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tonpa-shenrab-miwoche">Tonpa Shenrab Miwoche</a> and to the ancient kingdom of Zhangzhung in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>. Early Tibetan sources use the name for priests who performed royal funerary and other rites; the organised Bon religion that took shape from the eleventh century shares much with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhism</a>. It is practised today in Tibet, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bon.jpg" alt="Bon" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jialiang Gao (peace-on-earth.org) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Narshi_Gonpa_Ngawa_Sichuan_China.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
